# Smart Data Analyzer

> A full-stack web application built with Flask that lets you upload any CSV file and instantly get automated data analysis, 
visualizations, and machine learning insights — all in the browser.

I built this project to strengthen my understanding of how Python data libraries work together in a real web application. 
Instead of just running scripts in Jupyter Notebook, I wanted to see if I could take everything I had learned — Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, and Scikit-Learn — and actually ship something usable.

---

## 🚀 What It Does

Upload any CSV file and the app automatically:

- Profiles your dataset (rows, columns, data types, missing values, duplicates)
- Generates charts — feature distributions, correlation heatmap, missing value bars
- Runs **K-Means clustering** with **PCA** to group similar rows using Scikit-Learn
- Detects smart insights like skewed columns, strong correlations, and data quality issues
- Shows everything in a clean dark-themed dashboard — no setup needed by the user

---

## 🛠️ Tech Stack

| Layer | Technology |
|-------|-----------|
| Backend | Python, Flask |
| Data Processing | Pandas, NumPy |
| Visualization | Matplotlib |
| Machine Learning | Scikit-Learn (KMeans, PCA, StandardScaler) |
| Frontend | HTML5, CSS3, Vanilla JavaScript |

---

## 📁 Project Structure

```
smart_data_analyzer/
│
├── app.py                  # Flask routes and all analysis logic
├── requirements.txt        # Python dependencies
├── README.md
│
├── templates/
│   └── index.html          # Main UI (HTML only)
│
├── static/
│   ├── css/
│   │   └── style.css       # All styles
│   └── js/
│       └── main.js         # Frontend logic (upload, fetch, render)
│
└── uploads/                # Temporary storage for uploaded CSVs
```

---

## ⚙️ How to Run Locally

**1. Clone the repository**
```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/smart-data-analyzer.git
cd smart-data-analyzer
```

**2. Install dependencies**
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

**3. Start the app**
```bash
python app.py
```

**4. Open in your browser**
```
http://127.0.0.1:5000
```

---

## 📌 How to Use

1. Drag and drop any `.csv` file onto the upload zone (or click to browse)
2. Click **⚡ ANALYZE DATA**
3. Scroll through your results:
   - Overview stats at the top
   - Smart insights panel
   - Full column profile table
   - Top correlations list
   - Auto-generated charts and ML clustering plot

---

## 🧠 What I Learned Building This

This was my first time taking data analysis code out of a notebook and putting it inside a real web application. A few things I figured out along the way:

- How Flask routes work and how to send/receive JSON between the backend and frontend
- How to convert Matplotlib figures to base64 strings so they can be displayed in the browser without saving files
- How to use `StandardScaler` before running KMeans — I initially forgot this and the clustering results were completely wrong
- How to structure a Flask project properly with separate `templates/` and `static/` folders
- How the browser's `fetch()` API works for sending form data and rendering results dynamically

